From b2e8b7a9d3f6986950c8b2653ad221f7bde7efaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Junod <donujp@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 17:24:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/11] backends/color: Apply Night Light in linear light in
 BT.2100

Night Light programs blackbody scales into the CRTC gamma LUT, or the
CTM without one. That runs after composition, on encoded values. For a
gamma encoding this is the long accepted approximation, but in BT.2100
the values are PQ code values: scaling them removes far more light than
the scale, unequally across the tonal range, and skews hue.

With a PQ encoding, keep the gamma LUT and the CTM at identity, with no
temperature and no calibration ramp of a profile, which is made for
SDR. Instead, adapt the output's white to the Planckian white of the
Night Light temperature in the rendering color state, in linear light
before PQ encoding. It warms and very slightly dims, as in SDR, but
never brightens. The Planckian chromaticity uses Krystek's rational
approximation of the locus.

The color device sets it on the rendering color state next to the
target volume, so the renderer, direct scanout and the cursor plane
read the same state. At the default temperature there is no adaptation
and the rendering color state is exactly the one without Night Light.
Each temperature step is a new value of the same adaptation. Screen
casts and screenshots do not carry it, as they do not carry the gamma
LUT in SDR. SDR modes keep the existing behavior.

Related: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/issues/3687
